{"graph_chars": "Good morning. I am Professor Rei Natsume. This lecture is Chapter Four of Principles of Macroeconomics, OpenStax, third edition: Labor and Financial Markets. Chapter Three priced goods. This chapter uses the same demand and supply model on jobs and on saving and borrowing. The opening case is baby boomers. The two thousand twenty Census: twenty-two percent of the United States was sixty or older — more than seventy-four million people who will need more medical care. The group born between nineteen forty-six and nineteen sixty-four is more than seventy-one million. Aging, plus life-extending technology, plus the Affordable Care Act, raise demand for healthcare and for nurses. In labor markets, job seekers supply labor. Firms demand it. In financial markets, savers supply funds. Borrowers demand them. In two thousand eighteen, forty-three percent of full-time college students worked, and eighty-one percent of part-time students did. In two thousand eighteen to nineteen, forty-three percent of full-time undergraduates had loan aid averaging seventy-three hundred dollars a year. You are already in both markets.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0594], [0.0594, 0.1188], [0.1188, 0.1782], [0.1782, 0.2377], [0.2377, 0.2971], [0.2971, 0.3565], [0.3565, 0.4159], [0.4159, 0.4753], [0.4753, 0.5347], [0.5347, 0.5941], [0.5941, 0.6535], [0.6535, 0.713], [0.713, 0.7724], [0.7724, 0.8318], [0.8318, 0.8912], [0.8912, 0.9506], [0.9506, 1.01], [1.01, 1.0694], [1.0694, 1.1289], [1.1289, 1.1883], [1.1883, 1.2477], [1.2477, 1.3071], [1.3071, 1.3665], [1.3665, 1.4259], [1.4259, 1.4853], [1.4853, 1.5447], [1.5447, 1.6042], [1.6042, 1.6636], [1.6636, 1.723], [1.723, 1.7824], [1.7824, 1.8418], [1.8418, 1.9012], [1.9012, 1.9606], [1.9606, 2.0201], [2.0201, 2.0795], [2.0795, 2.1389], [2.1389, 2.1983], [2.1983, 2.2577], [2.2577, 2.3171], [2.3171, 2.3765], [2.3765, 2.4359], [2.4359, 2.4954], [2.4954, 2.5548], [2.5548, 2.6142], [2.6142, 2.6736], [2.6736, 2.733], [2.733, 2.7924], [2.792